WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.281
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.581 --> 00:00:09.106
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.406 --> 00:00:15.623
Unit fourteen: Archival Research. Today you will describe how sources are checked with the passive.

4
00:00:15.923 --> 00:00:18.529
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:22.676
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.517 --> 00:00:24.436
Archive.

7
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:28.518
Archive means a place where historical records are kept.

8
00:00:29.117 --> 00:00:31.929
The national archive opened its colonial files.

9
00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:34.809
Your turn. Say: Archive.

10
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:38.528
Archive.

11
00:00:39.283 --> 00:00:40.702
Primary source.

12
00:00:41.717 --> 00:00:45.214
Primary source means original material from the period studied.

13
00:00:45.817 --> 00:00:48.501
Letters and photographs are primary sources.

14
00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:52.257
Your turn. Say: Primary source.

15
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.275
Primary source.

16
00:00:57.017 --> 00:00:58.006
Provenance.

17
00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:03.617
Provenance means the origin and history of ownership of a document or object.

18
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:06.611
The statue's provenance was investigated.

19
00:01:07.217 --> 00:01:09.614
Your turn. Say: Provenance.

20
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:13.561
Provenance.

21
00:01:14.317 --> 00:01:15.353
Preservation.

22
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.766
Preservation means protecting materials from damage or decay.

23
00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.464
Humidity makes preservation difficult in Cambodia.

24
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.505
Your turn. Say: Preservation.

25
00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:30.472
Preservation.

26
00:01:31.217 --> 00:01:32.334
Access.

27
00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.743
Access means the right or opportunity to use something.

28
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.860
Researchers need permission for access.

29
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.951
Your turn. Say: Access.

30
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:46.929
Access.

31
00:01:47.683 --> 00:01:48.673
Catalog.

32
00:01:49.683 --> 00:01:53.076
Catalog means a systematic list of items in a collection.

33
00:01:53.683 --> 00:01:56.263
The catalog lists 20,000 documents.

34
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.293
Your turn. Say: Catalog.

35
00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:03.100
Catalog.

36
00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.014
Digitization.

37
00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:09.386
Digitization means converting documents into digital form.

38
00:02:09.983 --> 00:02:13.527
Digitization protects fragile palm-leaf manuscripts.

39
00:02:14.117 --> 00:02:16.885
Your turn. Say: Digitization.

40
00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:20.955
Digitization.

41
00:02:21.717 --> 00:02:23.391
Fragmentary evidence.

42
00:02:24.383 --> 00:02:28.252
Fragmentary evidence means evidence that survives only in pieces.

43
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:32.011
Historians rebuilt events from fragmentary evidence.

44
00:02:32.617 --> 00:02:36.186
Your turn. Say: Fragmentary evidence.

45
00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:40.603
Fragmentary evidence.

46
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.993
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:51.405
Visal, a museum abroad claims its Khmer statue was bought legally. How would a historian check that?

48
00:02:52.517 --> 00:03:04.039
The statue's provenance must be carefully verified. Every sale record should be examined, and photos from the 1960s compared with the temple site.

49
00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.161
What if the documents are incomplete?

50
00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:18.739
Then fragmentary evidence has to be pieced together. In several cases, statues were proven to have been looted, and they have been returned to Cambodia.

51
00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:22.697
And what about old palm-leaf manuscripts here?

52
00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:31.298
They are being digitized, so that the originals can be protected from humidity while scholars are given access online.

53
00:03:32.433 --> 00:03:34.189
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:38.849
Grammar focus: Passive for verification process.

55
00:03:39.149 --> 00:03:40.231
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:40.917 --> 00:03:43.822
A source's provenance must be carefully verified.

57
00:03:44.717 --> 00:03:47.413
Several statues have been returned to Cambodia.

58
00:03:48.317 --> 00:03:50.920
Palm-leaf manuscripts are being digitized.

59
00:03:51.867 --> 00:04:02.391
Research methods are described in the passive because the process matters more than the researcher: must be verified, were compared, are being digitized. Choose the tense carefully for each stage.

60
00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.917
Be careful! Don't say: The provenance must verified.

61
00:04:08.867 --> 00:04:11.865
Say: The provenance must be verified.

62
00:04:12.165 --> 00:04:13.990
Modal passive needs 'be'.

63
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.510
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.777
Inside the Archive.

65
00:04:20.077 --> 00:04:26.384
Archives are not neutral storehouses; what has been kept, and what has been lost, shapes what historians can know.

66
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.929
Before a document is used as evidence, its provenance must be established and its authenticity tested.

67
00:04:33.933 --> 00:04:40.542
In Cambodia, many records were destroyed during the conflict, so historians often work with fragmentary evidence.

68
00:04:41.533 --> 00:04:47.980
Today, collections are being digitized and catalogued, which greatly widens access for students and researchers.

69
00:04:48.967 --> 00:04:55.006
Careful archival work has even helped to identify looted artefacts and return them to their rightful communities.

70
00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.125
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:00.983 --> 00:05:03.877
The documents blank by experts before publication.

72
00:05:04.177 --> 00:05:08.028
A, were examined. B, examined. C, were examine.

73
00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:13.439
The answer is A: were examined.

74
00:05:14.583 --> 00:05:16.699
The manuscripts blank at the moment.

75
00:05:16.999 --> 00:05:21.871
A, are digitizing. B, are being digitized. C, being digitized.

76
00:05:25.433 --> 00:05:28.234
The answer is B: are being digitized.

77
00:05:29.383 --> 00:05:32.695
The origin and ownership history of an object is its blank.

78
00:05:32.995 --> 00:05:36.788
A, catalog. B, access. C, provenance.

79
00:05:40.333 --> 00:05:42.356
The answer is C: provenance.

80
00:05:43.517 --> 00:05:46.224
Evidence that survives only in pieces is blank.

81
00:05:46.524 --> 00:05:51.559
A, fragmentary evidence. B, primary source. C, preservation.

82
00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:58.017
The answer is A: fragmentary evidence.

83
00:05:59.183 --> 00:06:01.334
According to the reading, archives are…

84
00:06:01.634 --> 00:06:06.599
A, neutral storehouses. B, not neutral. C, always complete.

85
00:06:10.133 --> 00:06:12.365
The answer is B: not neutral.

86
00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:16.625
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:17.483 --> 00:06:21.352
Why is verifying a source's provenance important in historical research?

88
00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:39.685
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:39.985 --> 00:07:16.697
Verifying a source's provenance is important because a document or object can only be trusted as evidence if its origin is known. If a forged letter is accepted without being checked, an entire interpretation of the past may be built on a lie. Provenance research has also helped Cambodia, since several looted statues were identified and returned from foreign museums. Some people see this work as slow and technical. However, I believe careful verification is what separates history from rumour.

90
00:07:16.997 --> 00:07:18.776
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:19.683 --> 00:07:24.762
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive for verification process.

92
00:07:25.062 --> 00:07:29.978
Today's value is meticulousness. Check every detail carefully before you trust it.

93
00:07:30.278 --> 00:07:32.931
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:33.231 --> 00:07:35.616
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

